WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.000
- And so, I never went to
school, anything like that.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.270
Like I said, no brothers, no
sisters, no wife, no kids.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.103
- [Interviewer] No parents?

00:00:07.103 --> 00:00:08.250
- Huh?
- No parents, after your mom.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.200
- No parents, no, I never
knew my father at all,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.720
never saw him at all.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.203
And I flew from Cleveland.

00:00:20.896 --> 00:00:23.813
(breathes shakily)

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:29.556
I flew, Jacksonville, Florida,

00:00:29.556 --> 00:00:32.930
from Jacksonville to Cleveland,
Ohio to see this man.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.625
Where I was at the first time.

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:36.823
I flew back to Cleveland to see him.

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:40.863
(sniffles) Excuse me.

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:44.870
And my grandmother
called him on the phone.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.347
And told him, said, "Your
only son is in town,

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:49.953
"do you want to see him?"

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.940
He wouldn't drive a mile
to come see me in his car.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.940
I flew 3,000 miles to see him.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.000
- [Interviewer] You came
from Florida to Cleveland.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.340
- Yeah.
- And he wouldn't go a mile?

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.980
- He wouldn't get in his
car, not a mile, to see me.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.325
He told me to bring me down there!

00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:03.730
I said, "No, I'm not goin'."

00:01:04.886 --> 00:01:08.300
So we talked on the phone, (sniffles)

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.900
and I told him, I said,
"You know I don't do drugs?"

00:01:12.647 --> 00:01:15.137
He said, "That's a good thing."

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.917
I said, "I know it is."

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:21.407
I said, "You know, I don't do wild sex,

00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:23.480
"and I ain't got no girls pregnant."

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.777
I said, "You know, I'm pretty
built, and I look good."

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.307
He said, "Well, that's all good.

00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:30.150
"You gonna come down and see me?"

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.787
I said, "No, I'm not."

00:01:31.787 --> 00:01:35.462
And it is all good, 'cause my
mother taught me to do that.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.212
And I told 'im on the phone, I say,

00:01:38.257 --> 00:01:42.170
"It was a woman, that taught
me how to be a man," you know?

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.530
I got some tissue, I got
some baby wipes here, too,

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:45.766
yeah.
- Okay.

00:01:45.766 --> 00:01:48.397
- I said, "It was a woman who
taught me how to be a man.

00:01:48.397 --> 00:01:50.527
"And everything I
learned about being a man

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:51.947
"was from a woman."

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:54.561
And he wouldn't come see me,

00:01:54.561 --> 00:01:57.415
(sniffles)

00:01:57.415 --> 00:02:00.850
and so, they had bought
me a round-trip ticket,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.000
I got on the plane four days later,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.500
and flew back to Jacksonville.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.620
But I did see him once, right?

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.660
I remember seeing somebody,
but I was so young,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.480
I couldn't recognize what he looked like.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.770
You know?

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.105
But I remember seein' somebody once,

00:02:17.105 --> 00:02:19.973
when I was very, very, young, you know?

00:02:21.427 --> 00:02:24.260
But I never saw 'im after that no more.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.270
- Saw somebody that you
thought was your dad?

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:30.063
- Well, I saw a guy, I
remember him, a black guy.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.387
We was in a movie
theater, I remember that.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:35.943
And I was very young.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.133
He either worked there, or he owned it.

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:42.620
I think it was my dad, you know?

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.763
But my mom died so early, of cancer, so.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.520
I was like 12 years old when she died.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.170
But as far as the schooling goes,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.271
we traveled a lot, because my grandmother

00:02:55.271 --> 00:02:58.730
and my mother's fightin'
on who would raise me.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.623
And so she had to run away with me.

00:03:01.622 --> 00:03:04.700
And then by the time I got
maybe eight, nine, 10 years old,

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.250
they felt, my mother had cancer,

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.620
and wasn't able to take care o' me.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.990
So they put me in a foster home,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.743
I ran away from the foster home.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.820
Miss Clayton, I took the
garbage out one morning,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.423
she knew, she was a good lady.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.230
Gimme a minute here, 'cause--

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.170
- [Interviewer] Yeah, I know.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.310
- Sometime I get a little
emotional when I think.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.720
But I'm good spirit, but I'm sayin'

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.830
sometime when I think about it.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.430
'Cause she was a good woman,
but she knew I was gonna run,

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:33.891
when I went and took the
trash out one morning.

00:03:33.891 --> 00:03:35.840
She say, "Baby, come on back."

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.442
And I looked at 'er,
and I took off runnin'.

00:03:38.442 --> 00:03:39.307
- [Interviewer] Just in case.

00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:40.518
- I just walked down the street.

00:03:40.518 --> 00:03:43.505
I didn't run, I didn't, but
she knew I wasn't comin' back.

00:03:43.505 --> 00:03:47.410
'Cause I was there one day,
and the first day I was there--

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.330
- [Interviewer] This is the foster home?

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:49.163
- Yeah.

00:03:49.163 --> 00:03:51.180
The first day I was there,

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.080
I'm a fighter, right?

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.950
I never been a real coward, so,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.020
the first day I was
there, one o' my brothers,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.120
I had met him in there, my foster brother.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.133
He got in a fight.

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:02.710
I walked around the corner,
about 10, 15 guys was on 'im.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.730
Well, about three or
four of 'em was on him.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:06.563
And I turned around, and I asked him,

00:04:06.563 --> 00:04:09.350
I said, "Joshua," I say ...

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.575
he was a white boy, 'cause
it was a foster home, right?

00:04:12.575 --> 00:04:14.675
And I walked around, asked Joshua, I said,

00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:18.657
"What's wrong, Joshua, why
you standin' against the car

00:04:18.657 --> 00:04:21.430
"and all three of these guys
standin' in front o' you?"

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.300
And when he looked at
me, he wouldn't speak.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.577
And I stepped up, and
I said, "What's wrong?

00:04:27.577 --> 00:04:29.487
"Shit, you can talk to me."

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.653
And then one of the dudes
turned around, he said,

00:04:35.026 --> 00:04:36.440
"And what do you got to do with this?"

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.547
I said, "That's my brother."

00:04:38.547 --> 00:04:39.950
"He's a white boy, you're black,"

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.850
I said, "We in a foster home
together, that's my brother."

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:47.360
He say, "Well, this man owe us money."

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.060
And I say, "For what?"

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.860
He said, "Because you gotta
pay to live on this street."

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.707
I said, "I just moved here,
I'm not payin' you shit."

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:57.783
And I got down.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.727
And he said, "Well, we'll
drag you up against the car,"

00:05:00.727 --> 00:05:03.142
and I told 'im, I say, "I'm your guy."

00:05:03.142 --> 00:05:05.950
And when I went to fightin' these guys,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.410
I was doin' pretty good with
the three of 'em, right?

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.570
Then, for a while I
had a whole city block.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.710
People runnin' from
everywhere, women, and kids,

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.550
and he hit me in the head with bricks,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.529
this was in Jacksonville, Florida.

00:05:16.529 --> 00:05:18.500
And they was hittin' me
in the head with bricks,

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.692
and sticks, and when I
got home that evenin',

00:05:20.692 --> 00:05:23.060
shirt tore all up, Joshua ran.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.980
He went, but I didn't take it personally,

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.880
he ran, because he was
young, and I realized

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.470
he wasn't the type of guy I was, you know?

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:30.960
Everybody's not a fighter.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.150
Some people are very brainy and smart.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.250
Joshua was very smart, with
books, anything like that.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.910
(sniffs) So he ran, and when I got home,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.920
my shirt was tore up, and
my eyes looked swollen.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.457
Nothin' that real life-threatenin',

00:05:42.457 --> 00:05:44.770
but a little blood, and over here,

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.760
I think I had to get three, four stitches

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:47.856
in the head with some people

00:05:47.856 --> 00:05:50.106
that hit me in the head
with bricks and shit.

00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:52.797
And so Miss Clayton, she said,

00:05:52.797 --> 00:05:54.454
"What happened to you?"

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:55.810
I said, "Aw, nothin'," I didn't tell 'er

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.037
I was savin' Joshua, I said, "Aw, nothin',

00:05:58.037 --> 00:06:01.501
"I was just out there playin',
nothin' to worry about."

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:02.334
And she looked at my head, and said,

00:06:02.334 --> 00:06:03.167
"Oh, your head's bleedin' so bad,

00:06:03.167 --> 00:06:04.546
"we gonna take you to the hospital."

00:06:04.546 --> 00:06:06.294
They put three, four stitches in there.

00:06:06.294 --> 00:06:08.403
But the next mornin' when I woke up,

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:10.870
I couldn't live like that, you know?

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.233
So I took the garbage out,
and she knew I was gonna run.

00:06:14.233 --> 00:06:19.233
And I ran, I walk away, and
she knew I wasn't comin' back.

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:23.147
And they put my face on
milk cartons and stuff,

00:06:23.147 --> 00:06:24.850
talkin' about, "Have you seen this kid,"

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:28.670
and made it like I was from
a rich family or somethin',

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.369
they were lookin' for me, and I wasn't.

00:06:30.369 --> 00:06:31.680
(laughs) They just
wanted to find me quick.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.963
- [Interviewer] So, you
lost your mom at what age?

00:06:33.963 --> 00:06:35.150
- About 12 years.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.050
I never saw 'er again after I left.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.610
I didn't, I think, once they
put me in a foster home,

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.480
they were runnin', lookin' for me so much.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.033
I was raised, well, I stayed only with her

00:06:44.033 --> 00:06:45.557
till I was about 12, 13 years old.

00:06:45.557 --> 00:06:46.930
- [Interviewer] And she died of cancer?

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:47.960
- Cancer, uh-huh.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.200
And so then they put me
in a, when they caught me,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.163
they put me in like a group home,

00:06:58.813 --> 00:06:59.850
called Trenton.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:03.360
One was called Trenton, and
one was called Marianna.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.460
It was for boys had done crime.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.133
But I had never did no crime.

00:07:07.133 --> 00:07:10.320
I was just, you know, runnin'
from foster homes and stuff.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.410
- [Interviewer] Be careful with eating.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.253
Just hold off--
- Oh, okay, I'm sorry, yeah.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:16.660
And I was runnin' from
foster homes and stuff,

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:20.160
so I never really did no crime,
but they put me in there.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.130
And I didn't know how to read or nothin'.

00:07:24.766 --> 00:07:26.490
I didn't know how to read,
and then so I started,

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.237
you know, walkin' around
lookin' at store signs, and--

00:07:29.237 --> 00:07:31.560
- [Interviewer] So, how
much schooling did you get?

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.253
- Oh, pretty, hardly none.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.930
I think I went to kindergarten
for about two months,

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.775
and maybe elementary about a month.

00:07:40.775 --> 00:07:43.330
I never really, I don't even remember.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:44.163
- [Interviewer] You never saw high school.

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:45.134
- No, no, no, no.
- You never saw,

00:07:45.134 --> 00:07:46.126
even grade school.

00:07:46.126 --> 00:07:47.283
- No, no, no.

00:07:47.283 --> 00:07:48.960
- [Interviewer] But you
taught yourself to read.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:49.940
- Yeah, uh-huh.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.550
I taught myself to read by walkin'

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.293
and lookin' at signs and stuff.

00:07:53.293 --> 00:07:57.440
Just standin' there, just
wait, like FedEx, you know?

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:58.668
And I would figure it out.

00:07:58.668 --> 00:08:02.070
And I would just figure it
out, I kept tryin' and tryin',

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.453
like Annie bean, Annie's Bean.

00:08:05.844 --> 00:08:07.890
You know, I kept figurin' it out.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.200
And I got messed over a
lot, I went to jail a lot,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.427
for things, I mean, prison a lot,

00:08:11.427 --> 00:08:15.260
'cause I couldn't read
contracts and stuff like that.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.100
You know, like one time, I stole a radio.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.340
I was just, wanted to get
somethin' to eat, you know?

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:22.805
I was in Oklahoma, and there
was a radio on the car seat.

00:08:22.805 --> 00:08:24.410
I just reached and grabbed,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.450
that's a small misdemeanor, right?

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.140
First time I'd ever been in trouble,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.618
and they sent me to prison
for three years for that.

00:08:30.618 --> 00:08:33.234
And then, I need to tell you something,

00:08:33.234 --> 00:08:36.390
off the record, though,
when you're, off the camera.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.373
- Okay.
- 'Cause that's kinda private.

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:45.290
And so, I kept goin' to jail,
and I was learnin' to read,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.020
I was gettin' better,
but I kept goin' to jail.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.450
Not because I was a hell of a lawbreaker,

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.610
but because I didn't have nobody to,

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.410
no lawyer, no money, no family or nothin'.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.200
And I could tell, from the
way that the things I did,

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.681
all the stuff goin' on in the world,

00:09:01.681 --> 00:09:03.450
I knew, I had the knowledge, and knew

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:04.353
that you were messin' me over.

00:09:04.353 --> 00:09:06.802
It's just, I couldn't do nothin' about it.

00:09:06.802 --> 00:09:10.130
'Cause I was so young,
and I didn't understand,

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.876
you know, a lot of things
back then, the courts.

00:09:12.876 --> 00:09:14.670
So, I mean, that's not, that's just

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.220
the way life is sometime, you know?

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:19.300
You can't be bitter about
that when you grow up,

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.330
'cause, you know, I tell a
lotta people we never gonna live

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.903
in a problem-free world,
see what I'm sayin'?

00:09:23.903 --> 00:09:25.853
There's always gonna be a problem.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.670
To every problem there's a
solution, but to every solution,

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.040
there's another problem occurs, you know?

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.050
So, we have to learn to live with that.

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:37.710
And, yeah, I got raped one time.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.302
I got, tried to rape me one
time, in Cleveland, Ohio.

00:09:41.302 --> 00:09:44.866
He took me in the butt, and
tried to put a fork to my neck.

00:09:44.866 --> 00:09:49.130
And I was young, and I
jumped out the window

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.770
with my underwear on, and
ran down the alleyway.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:52.634
- [Interviewer] How old were you?

00:09:52.634 --> 00:09:57.580
- I was about, shoot, I was
probably about 13 or 14 then.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.010
Somewhere, I don't remember.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.740
But I was very young, I
wasn't even 15 at that time.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.439
So I know I was either 14 or younger.

00:10:04.439 --> 00:10:07.545
And I tried to tell the police about it,

00:10:07.545 --> 00:10:09.780
and they talked me out
of it, in Cleveland.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.337
They said, "Well, you sure

00:10:11.337 --> 00:10:14.677
"you wanna press charges on this guy?

00:10:14.677 --> 00:10:16.437
"Go to court, and tell 'em all that stuff

00:10:16.437 --> 00:10:18.757
"in the courtroom, and, you know?

00:10:18.757 --> 00:10:21.067
"You maybe just oughta
let it go, you know."

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:23.510
I didn't want to, but I did,

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.557
'cause I was young, and they made me think

00:10:25.557 --> 00:10:28.340
that I would be embarrassed
in the courtroom,

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.270
and I had to tell all this stuff.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:32.730
So, I never reported it.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.343
And then one time, I was in LA.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.093
I was homeless, in Hollywood.

00:10:39.534 --> 00:10:42.030
And out came one o' the
most beautiful girls

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.380
I had ever seen in my
life, her name was Mytelle.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.070
She was a white girl, long blonde hair

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.833
all the way down to damn
near her kneecaps, her knees.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.320
And she was standin' in front
of one o' the roughest part

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.890
of Hollywood's back then, it was rough.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.783
And real nasty back then,
full o' gangbangers,

00:10:57.783 --> 00:10:59.870
and full of rough guys, like, you know,

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.660
we had to be rougher than
the roughest back then.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.940
'Cause this was like in
the '80s, early '80s.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.100
And didn't nobody care.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.980
But anyway, to make a long story short,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.699
I had a friend, worked
for the police department,

00:11:12.699 --> 00:11:15.200
and Mytelle wouldn't tell
me where she was from.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.390
But she was very beautiful.

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.680
And I wanted, she liked me, and I wanted,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.660
when we stayed together and she
wanted to be with me, right?

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:23.667
And I wanted to, but I thought,

00:11:23.667 --> 00:11:25.090
"Got to find out who I'm
sleepin' with, you know?"

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:26.683
I said, "I can't do that till I find out

00:11:26.683 --> 00:11:29.220
"who this girl is," you
know, she had no ID.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.510
So I gave her picture

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.560
to a buddy o' mine at the police station.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:36.737
He came back with a poster,
and she was facin' this way,

00:11:36.737 --> 00:11:39.910
and that way, with all these
things in front of her.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.007
And I thought, "God damn it,

00:11:41.007 --> 00:11:43.560
"this beautiful woman a criminal?"

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:44.810
Well, he said she wasn't.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:49.710
So, on the bottom o' the
poster, said "call this number."

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.430
I called the number
and it was her parents.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:56.319
And I said, "You have a
daughter named Mytelle?"

00:11:56.319 --> 00:11:59.177
He said, "Yes, we lookin' for her,

00:11:59.177 --> 00:12:02.060
"she's very young, and
she ran away from home."

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:04.960
I said, "Well, she's out here
in Los Angeles, California."

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.997
Nah, I said, "She's out here in a suburb

00:12:08.997 --> 00:12:12.130
"of Los Angeles, California,
called Hollywood."

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:15.400
He said, "Yes, yes," he
said, "We want her home!"

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.637
I said, "Okay.

00:12:16.637 --> 00:12:18.257
"I'm gonna put her on a bus,

00:12:18.257 --> 00:12:19.647
"and send her back to your home.

00:12:19.647 --> 00:12:22.430
"You be at the bus
station to pick her up."

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:23.917
He said, "No, no, no,
no, you can't do that,

00:12:23.917 --> 00:12:26.037
"she'll jump off the bus!"

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:29.257
I said, "And so what
do you want me to do?"

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.190
He said, "We'll pay you,
bring her back out here."

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.690
She had to go to Kenosha, Wisconsin.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:37.670
One of the suburbs, I mean, a city,

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.070
right off the border of Illinois.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:41.416
It's supposed to be the
last little city in--

00:12:41.416 --> 00:12:42.600
- [Interviewer] Yep, I know Kenosha.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.243
- Yeah, see, I had to take
her to Kenosha, Wisconsin.

00:12:46.243 --> 00:12:48.130
But he said, "Bring 'er back!"

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:50.780
And I was like, I said, "You want me?"

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:52.542
(laughs) That's all I
said, I said, "You want me,

00:12:52.542 --> 00:12:54.417
"to leave Los Angeles right now,

00:12:54.417 --> 00:12:56.387
"come all the way out to Chicago,

00:12:56.387 --> 00:12:57.917
"to bring your daughter home?"

00:12:59.909 --> 00:13:02.317
I said, "That ain't gonna happen, sir.

00:13:02.317 --> 00:13:03.927
"I just can't pull up like that,

00:13:03.927 --> 00:13:06.850
"and just leave Los Angeles like that."

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:10.487
He say, "Well, would
$2,000 make you do that?"

00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:15.770
(laughs) I said, "Come again?"

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.007
He say, "I'll pay you two grand,

00:13:17.007 --> 00:13:18.457
"you bring my daughter home."

00:13:20.697 --> 00:13:21.973
And so I said, "Okay,

00:13:23.547 --> 00:13:26.459
"I guess I'm goin' on a trip!" (giggles)

00:13:26.459 --> 00:13:27.900
And I jumped on the Greyhound,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.600
and we rode all the way the Greyhound.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.560
She cried, and didn't wanna go back,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.587
wanted to stay, and I said, "I can't.

00:13:33.587 --> 00:13:35.957
"It's too rough out there, you know,

00:13:35.957 --> 00:13:37.767
"I have no way of takin' care o' you,

00:13:37.767 --> 00:13:39.367
"and, you know, it's too dangerous,

00:13:39.367 --> 00:13:41.143
"everybody's killin' everybody,

00:13:42.237 --> 00:13:44.060
"this is not the place for you right now."

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:48.160
And so, she cried, I got her
to the Chicago bus station.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.630
I took her to Chicago,
I didn't actually go

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.933
to Kenosha, Wisconsin,
they met us in Chicago.

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:55.967
And so, when they got in Chicago,

00:13:55.967 --> 00:13:59.187
her parents, they looked at me like,

00:13:59.187 --> 00:14:01.190
"Boy, (chuckles) this a
rough-lookin dude here!"

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:03.408
'Cause I mean, I was dressed
nice, had my jeans on,

00:14:03.408 --> 00:14:05.750
and back at this time I had
a pair of steel-toe boots,

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:08.880
the kind with the, had the
overlap over, the steel over it?

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:10.504
And then, you know, I'm wearin' shorts

00:14:10.504 --> 00:14:11.857
when I come out, not pants.

00:14:11.857 --> 00:14:13.620
And it's cold out there!

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:14.803
But it was hot in Los Angeles.

00:14:14.803 --> 00:14:17.740
So I went, and see all my
veins stickin' out o' my legs,

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.930
and, you know, I got
a jacket, a hoodie on,

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:21.790
but I got a hoodie on with a zip-down.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.187
I didn't have a necklace at the time.

00:14:23.187 --> 00:14:25.750
But I had a hoodie on, and
I was really bigger then,

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:27.490
you know, I'm just kinda cut now.

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:28.877
But I had a little size then.

00:14:28.877 --> 00:14:31.977
And so they looked at me like,
"Boy, this dude look rough."

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:37.070
So, I walked up to 'er, and
they saw Mytelle walk into us.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:38.837
And I said, "Yes, this is your daughter.

00:14:38.837 --> 00:14:42.343
"Nobody have ever touched
'er, and she's back home."

00:14:42.343 --> 00:14:44.400
And she cried, she wanted to stay with me,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.120
cried in that Chicago bus station.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:49.827
And I said, "Damn, boy, that bus ride,

00:14:49.827 --> 00:14:50.897
"I gotta take a long bus ride back."

00:14:50.897 --> 00:14:53.337
And they said, "No, we're
gonna get you a motel

00:14:53.337 --> 00:14:56.541
"for a couple days, and
we gonna get your $2,000,

00:14:56.541 --> 00:14:58.292
"but we'll get you on Amtrak."

00:14:58.292 --> 00:15:00.777
I said, "Well, how you gonna do all that?

00:15:00.777 --> 00:15:02.677
"I'm not gonna stay around with y'all.

00:15:03.852 --> 00:15:05.937
"And I'm not goin' out to your house.

00:15:05.937 --> 00:15:07.460
"Never go to a house I
ain't never been to before

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:10.620
"with people I don't know," you know?

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:12.097
And he said, "No, we'll get you a motel,

00:15:12.097 --> 00:15:14.728
"we'll just go home, we gonna
make sure you get the room,

00:15:14.728 --> 00:15:16.423
"and we're gonna get your money,

00:15:17.464 --> 00:15:19.827
"get you enough money to
eat on, and stuff like that,

00:15:19.827 --> 00:15:21.777
"and we gonna get you enough
money to buy you a ticket.

00:15:21.777 --> 00:15:25.937
"If you don't feel that relaxed, you know,

00:15:25.937 --> 00:15:26.980
"bein' among the people you don't know,"

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:29.593
he said, "I understand, you know."

00:15:29.593 --> 00:15:31.800
And he said, "I'm glad
you brought my daughter."

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.250
They took care of all that.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.930
So I got on the Amtrak,

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:38.780
the first time I ever rode a Amtrak train.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:40.630
And I got on the Amtrak, came back to Los,

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.770
and they had these big-ass windows.

00:15:42.770 --> 00:15:44.675
And you can drink on the Amtrak.

00:15:44.675 --> 00:15:47.680
And goin' on the Amtrak,
took us through Colorado.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:50.370
And what I did not know is that

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.580
all the time, way up in
the Colorado mountains,

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:57.603
I'd never saw a snow-white buck.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.630
But I saw a snow-white
buck with a pink nose!

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.730
And I said, "How'd that happen?"

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:07.067
He say, "We don't get much sun

00:16:07.067 --> 00:16:08.847
"up here, 'cause it gets too cold.

00:16:08.847 --> 00:16:10.240
"And the bucks don't get no"--

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.600
- Yeah, they're albino.
- Don't get that much sun.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:13.657
I say, "But snow-white?

00:16:13.657 --> 00:16:15.070
"They're not half-brown!" (laughs)

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:16.962
I said, "You get some sun!"

00:16:16.962 --> 00:16:17.850
- [Interviewer] Yeah, there's

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:19.102
white deer all over.
- They're snow-white.

00:16:19.102 --> 00:16:21.277
Yeah, he said, "They're bucks."

00:16:21.277 --> 00:16:25.233
And all that time, oh,
one thing I forgot--

00:16:25.233 --> 00:16:27.135
- Tell me about the girl.
- Huh?

00:16:27.135 --> 00:16:28.280
- [Interviewer] Did you ever
hear from the girl again?

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:30.380
- No, I never did hear
from her again, uh-uh.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.740
I never did hear from Mytelle no more.

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:34.410
Which was a good thing,
because I could tell

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.066
when I talked to her, she's not
ready for this kind of life,

00:16:37.066 --> 00:16:39.110
and I don't know if she would ever be.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.900
Mytelle was very
intelligent, and very smart.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.970
But she's like a girl that
would be in a college,

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:47.117
or a good job, she's not street smart.

00:16:47.117 --> 00:16:49.720
And out here, they would've
probably raped her,

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.710
gave her AIDS, and she'd have
been pregnant anytime soon,

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.806
and whatever career she had on her mind

00:16:54.806 --> 00:16:57.380
woulda been messed up, you know?

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.190
But now, one thing I forgot to tell you

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:00.910
when I was takin' her back.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.480
On the way down, me and
Mytelle got on the right bus,

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.430
'cause there was two Greyhound
buses, they got too full.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:08.707
The first one, ahead of us,
ran off the side of a cliff

00:17:08.707 --> 00:17:10.240
and killed everybody on the bus.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:11.073
- [Interviewer] Oh, you're kidding.

00:17:11.073 --> 00:17:12.790
- No, the one in front, it went over.

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:15.330
We got on the second bus.
- Wow.

00:17:15.330 --> 00:17:18.180
- If we'd've been on the
first bus, we woulda died.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:21.050
'Cause it was snowy, and icy
road, or somethin' like that,

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:23.032
and one o' them Greyhound
buses ran off the side

00:17:23.032 --> 00:17:26.030
o' the thing.
- Oh, jeez.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:27.640
- Some kinda ravine.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.316
I didn't see, but it landed
on some kinda steep edge.

00:17:30.316 --> 00:17:32.950
And from what I heard from what they said

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.240
about the bus, everybody on the bus died.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.490
Everybody on that first
Greyhound got killed.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.660
And then, so, once I got back to LA,

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.380
you know, I got back in the street.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.560
All this time, I was always
homeless with no family,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:46.300
when I'm goin' through this, so.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:48.450
I don't know how I got
into all these experiences,

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:51.500
but, and I lived in
many different cultures.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.370
I was homeless in, New York,
where it's very cold there.

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.500
I was homeless in
Cleveland, and I made my way

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.400
to Seattle, Washington,
I was homeless there.

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:02.330
- [Interviewer] And you're
entirely self-taught.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:03.330
- Yeah, and so--

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:04.163
- [Interviewer] No schooling.

00:18:04.163 --> 00:18:06.146
- No schoolin', uh-uh.

00:18:06.146 --> 00:18:09.580
Just among, you know, learning
from people, you know.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:11.790
Lookin at they behavior,
and the way they do things,

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.710
and just payin' attention to life,

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:18.377
but no schoolin', no tutorin',
no teachin' or nothin' like--

00:18:18.377 --> 00:18:19.873
- [Interviewer] And how old are you?

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.360
- Uh, 53, now, I just turned--
- Oh, 53.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.159
- I had a birthday two weeks
ago, three weeks ago, yeah.

00:18:26.159 --> 00:18:31.159
And so, no, no schoolin', no tutorin'.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.560
And, matter of fact, as I learnt,

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:39.317
a lot o' people out here say,
"How did you learn all that?

00:18:39.317 --> 00:18:40.767
"To hear you speak, you know,

00:18:40.767 --> 00:18:42.559
"I listen to the things you say,"

00:18:42.559 --> 00:18:45.402
and a lotta the guys on the street

00:18:45.402 --> 00:18:47.410
try to get me to teach them!

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:52.410
And I say, "Do you think
I can teach you anything?"

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.957
He say, "Yeah, I hear
you talk, and," I say,

00:18:55.957 --> 00:18:58.132
"Do you know I don't have
one day of school in myself?

00:18:58.132 --> 00:18:59.630
"How I'm gonna teach you?"

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:01.081
He said, "Well, just teach,"
they were lookin' at me,

00:19:01.081 --> 00:19:01.914
and said, "Well just teach us

00:19:01.914 --> 00:19:04.317
"what you talkin' about right there.

00:19:04.317 --> 00:19:06.417
"We ain't askin' you to
give us no education!

00:19:06.417 --> 00:19:08.931
"We just wanna learn some
o' the stuff you know."

00:19:08.931 --> 00:19:11.400
I said, "Okay, I'll do the best I can."

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:14.750
And so I did that, you
know, I would help guys.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:16.160
Even the guys around here now,

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:18.570
even the young dude
when they hear me talk.

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.140
Some of 'em be ready to beat me up,

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:22.190
and then, (laughs) this
one big guy, always

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:23.137
look and me and say, "I keep forgettin'

00:19:23.137 --> 00:19:24.777
"to beat that little mother," he say,

00:19:24.777 --> 00:19:26.690
"but he always right, man, you know?"

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:27.817
He say, "We wanna kick his ass,

00:19:27.817 --> 00:19:30.350
"he talk too much, but he always right."

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:33.438
- [Interviewer] So, you
had 12 years with your mom.

00:19:33.438 --> 00:19:34.680
- Yeah.
- How was your relationship

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:35.513
with your mom?

00:19:36.424 --> 00:19:38.273
- Me and my mom, we got along real good

00:19:38.273 --> 00:19:40.390
while she was livin', but she was so sick

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:43.330
all the time, with cancer, you know.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:44.703
That's another reason
I didn't go to school,

00:19:44.703 --> 00:19:45.940
I had to take care o'
her, I had to stay home

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:47.550
and take care o' her.
- Oh.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:50.343
- Because she was in the
hospital for a long time,

00:19:50.343 --> 00:19:53.900
for a while, but we couldn't
afford the hospital bill.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:56.794
So now, I had to stay
home and take care o' her.

00:19:56.794 --> 00:19:58.080
- [Interviewer] You're her only child?

00:19:58.080 --> 00:19:59.294
- Yeah.

00:19:59.294 --> 00:20:02.240
Until they took me to
put me in a foster home.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:05.480
And once they took me there,
I never got to see 'er again.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:08.900
She died, before I'd ever
see her again, you know.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.557
And then, I went to prison,

00:20:11.557 --> 00:20:13.907
up here in California for a while.

00:20:13.907 --> 00:20:17.483
And what happened, the way
I found out about her again,

00:20:19.001 --> 00:20:21.740
I'd never met none o' her
sisters or cousins, right?

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:24.193
But they had a missing
person report out on me,

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:27.117
my mom's only child, right?

00:20:27.117 --> 00:20:29.120
And when I got outta prison here,

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:30.557
the cops at one o' the
stations told me, he said,

00:20:30.557 --> 00:20:33.320
"You know you got a missing
person report out on you?"

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:36.640
I said, "I have no family to be missin'."

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:38.257
He said, "Somebody out
there lookin' for you,

00:20:38.257 --> 00:20:39.807
"and they from Cleveland, Ohio.

00:20:40.647 --> 00:20:41.777
"Now, I don't know if
you got family or not,

00:20:41.777 --> 00:20:43.017
"but there's a missing person,

00:20:43.017 --> 00:20:45.919
"somebody there's lookin' for you."

00:20:45.919 --> 00:20:46.810
So I said, "All right."

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:49.877
And he gave me a number,
and I called the number.

00:20:49.877 --> 00:20:50.710
And this woman get on the phone,

00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:53.080
she said, "I'm your aunt Antoinette.

00:20:54.137 --> 00:20:58.843
"We looked for you for years,
my sister's only child."

00:20:58.843 --> 00:21:00.897
She said, "We looked for you for years."

00:21:03.297 --> 00:21:05.517
(sniffles) "And we couldn't find you."

00:21:07.284 --> 00:21:09.027
And I said, "Where's my mother?"

00:21:11.074 --> 00:21:12.857
She said, "Your mother died."

00:21:17.097 --> 00:21:19.973
And I flew to Cleveland, for a third time.

00:21:21.623 --> 00:21:24.750
And then I found out she
wasn't buried in Cleveland,

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:28.737
so I had to go to Georgia,
where her father was.

00:21:28.737 --> 00:21:33.368
And I met my grandfather,
for the first time.

00:21:33.368 --> 00:21:35.810
I only stayed about a week. (sniffles)

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:36.910
And I said, "What
cemetery my mother buried,

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:40.220
"I wanna go see the grave." (sniffs)

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:42.590
And they buried her way out in the woods.

00:21:44.625 --> 00:21:45.680
'Cause they didn't have money

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:47.093
to put 'er in the good cemetery.

00:21:50.387 --> 00:21:52.322
"It's by the tree that
grows on her grave,"

00:21:52.322 --> 00:21:53.267
we had to find it.

00:21:53.267 --> 00:21:55.550
We had to dig through
the trees, the grave.

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:59.277
And I thought, "This what
you do to your daughter?"

00:22:01.192 --> 00:22:02.025
(sniffs) And I told my auntie, I said,

00:22:02.025 --> 00:22:04.317
"This what you do to y'all sister.

00:22:04.317 --> 00:22:06.272
"You bury 'er way out there
where nobody see 'er."

00:22:06.272 --> 00:22:07.937
The trees. (whimpers)

00:22:07.937 --> 00:22:12.840
They grew all over.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:17.213
And so, I left. (sniffles)

00:22:17.213 --> 00:22:20.367
After I saw my mother's grave,

00:22:20.367 --> 00:22:22.750
and the way they treated their sister,

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.857
and his daughter, I never
wanna see 'em no more.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:28.830
I left, and I never went back.

00:22:34.148 --> 00:22:38.272
It was just a hard life. (sniffs)

00:22:38.272 --> 00:22:39.570
For her.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:41.337
I didn't care so much about myself.

00:22:42.486 --> 00:22:44.786
She was always sick, and
she was a good woman.

00:22:46.883 --> 00:22:49.297
(sniffs) It was a hard life for 'er.

00:22:51.427 --> 00:22:53.513
(blowing nose) Excuse me.

00:22:55.984 --> 00:22:58.270
And I'm not a very big guy now, so.

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.700
I'm out here being the only
child, and a very small guy.

00:23:06.168 --> 00:23:07.920
And no schoolin', nobody to teach me,

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.260
I had to learn the best way I could.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:11.960
And I start pickin' up old things.

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:17.917
And I told myself, "Your
mother's in her grave,

00:23:17.917 --> 00:23:19.037
"and your grandmother gone, too,

00:23:19.037 --> 00:23:20.927
"so you not gonna grow up ignorant.

00:23:20.927 --> 00:23:24.193
"And you not gonna grow
up not havin' a life.

00:23:25.187 --> 00:23:26.087
"And you gonna teach yourself,

00:23:26.087 --> 00:23:28.033
"and you gonna do whatever you can.

00:23:29.491 --> 00:23:31.697
"And you gonna work hard,
because you will not be a loser.

00:23:31.697 --> 00:23:34.187
"You might be po'. (sniffs)

00:23:34.187 --> 00:23:36.470
"But you're not gonna be a loser."

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.220
And I fought hard, and
this is where it land me.

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:40.500
And I'm not mad at life.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:42.647
I'm happy, and just, some o' the things

00:23:42.647 --> 00:23:45.100
that I think about, in the past,

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:47.610
it makes me a little emotional.

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:48.963
But I've had a good life.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.010
And I'm proud o' my life, and,

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.277
I never really was materialistic anyway.

00:23:55.277 --> 00:23:59.393
I never had a driver
license or phone, a house.

00:23:59.393 --> 00:24:02.710
I never had, I haven't
touched a woman in 14 years.

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:05.475
I'm not gay, I'm disease-free,

00:24:05.475 --> 00:24:07.410
I never had no tattoos, no pierced ears,

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:08.640
or any o' that, you know?

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:09.920
- [Interviewer] And I
see you all the time,

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:11.880
I've seen you hundreds of
times, you're always happy.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:12.940
- Yeah, I'm always happy--

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:13.773
- [Interviewer] You're
always in a good mood.

00:24:13.773 --> 00:24:17.860
- Yeah, because, (grunts) excuse me.

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:19.127
Bein' in a bad mood ain't gonna do nothin'

00:24:19.127 --> 00:24:21.020
but make it worse, and it's not the world,

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:22.577
I don't feel like it's
the world's problem.

00:24:22.577 --> 00:24:23.660
- [Interviewer] But you're honest.

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:24.950
- Yeah.
- You're hard-working.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:26.010
- Right.
- You're a good guy,

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:27.500
you help out people that need help.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:28.760
- Yes, I do.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.217
Anytime.

00:24:30.217 --> 00:24:31.870
(laughing) I gave about, shoot,

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:34.065
almost half a pack o'
cigarettes to one everybody,

00:24:34.065 --> 00:24:35.230
got no money, "Can I
get one, can I get one?"

00:24:35.230 --> 00:24:36.063
- [Interviewer] Yeah, you're like

00:24:36.063 --> 00:24:36.896
the mayor of Skid Row down here.

00:24:36.896 --> 00:24:37.729
- Yeah, pretty much.

00:24:37.729 --> 00:24:39.720
Me and a other guy, you know, me and him,

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:41.850
we a different type o' characters,

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:46.850
but, you know, live the
same, he help people, too.

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:49.620
But you know, our entire
personalities a little different.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.860
But other than that, you
know, we about the same.

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:54.550
So, that's it right
there for me right now.

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:56.161
- Excellent.
- I know you got things to do,

00:24:56.161 --> 00:24:57.640
and you probably got
other people comin' in,

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:00.550
so we gonna cut it short.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:01.724
And to the world, thank you,

00:25:01.724 --> 00:25:06.724
and whatever you see on
this, I hope you enjoy.

00:25:06.966 --> 00:25:08.080
(Interviewer laughing)
It's only for your

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:11.480
entertainment, and for your listening.

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.783
So, thank y'all out there,
and have a good life.

00:25:13.783 --> 00:25:15.254
- [Interviewer] All right, Cigarette Man.

00:25:15.254 --> 00:25:16.779
- [Cigarette Man] (laughs) All right.

00:25:16.779 --> 00:25:18.070
(sighs)
- That was great.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:19.377
So that was a three-part video.

00:25:19.377 --> 00:25:20.210
(Cigarette Man laughs)
I was taking

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:21.260
your two-hour talk, and I turned it--

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:22.920
- Oh, I'm sorry!
- No, no, no, no, no, no!

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:23.890
This was the best one!

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:25.522
- Oh, okay--
- It's all good.

00:25:25.522 --> 00:25:28.076
Everything you share
is always interesting.

00:25:28.076 --> 00:25:29.147
- Yeah.
- 'Cause you're

00:25:29.147 --> 00:25:30.345
